  "response": "Disputes of this kind usually begin as a disagreement about attribution. The model developer attributed the benchmark gains to improved chain-of-thought training; only afterwards did independent researchers attribute those same gains to contamination of the test set. Stated carefully, the empirical question beneath the quarrel admits of exactly two answers, one of which must hold and both of which cannot: either the benchmark's test items appear verbatim in the model's pretraining corpus, or no test item from the benchmark appears verbatim in that corpus.\n\nThe first instrument brought to bear is corpus search. Researchers measure n-gram overlap between benchmark items and pretraining corpora to detect contamination, and a long run of matching n-grams is precisely what shows the first of those two possibilities to obtain, namely that the items sit in the pretraining data word for word. Whereas overlap statistics of this sort are straightforward to compute wherever the data can be opened, closed training corpora prevent external researchers from directly verifying contamination. That foreclosure of direct audit has in turn led evaluators to build fresh test material whose provenance they themselves control. Scale AI constructed GSM1k as a freshly written held-out benchmark mirroring the distribution of GSM8K. The GSM1k evaluation showed that every model family tested overfit GSM8K by more than ten accuracy points.\n\nA second strategy perturbs an existing benchmark rather than replacing it outright. GSM-Symbolic generates symbolic templates that vary the names and numeric values in GSM8K problems, and this innovation led to the convention whereby a drop in accuracy on perturbed variants of a benchmark is treated as evidence of memorization. Such a decline indicates that the model retained the specific answers to the benchmark's individual items. Retention of that kind does not, however, settle the larger question, for at least one of two things is true of a system that posts strong scores, and perhaps both at once: the item-level retention just described, or the model acquired a generalizable solution procedure for this class of problems.\n\nA third family of methods interrogates the model's own likelihoods instead of its answers. Min-K% Prob scores a sequence using the average log-probability of its least likely tokens. Perplexity on a contaminated example runs higher than perplexity on an unseen example of equal difficulty. Membership-inference scores of this kind are attractive because they require only query access, which is often all that an outside auditor has.\n\nNo single probe is decisive on its own. Verbatim-overlap searches, freshly authored held-out sets, symbolic perturbation and likelihood-based membership tests each fail in a different direction, and the field's working practice is therefore to demand convergence among them before crediting a reported gain to reasoning rather than to recall.",
